## 1. Introduction
Enzyme, a kind of catalytic protein, plays a key role in biological transformations with many excellent characteristics such as high efficiency, specificity, and selectivity [1–3]. Up to now, enzymes have been extensively used in food, chemistry, and pharmaceutical fields [4–6]. Nevertheless, it still remains a challenge for most of the enzymes to achieve theoretical results in practice application because of the poor stability, difficult recycling, and high cost $[7-$ 9]. In the past twenty years, enzyme immobilization has evolved into an effective method to increase the activity and stability of enzymes at extreme pH and high temperatures [10]. A variety of porous materials including silica composites, sol-gels, and membranes are used to immobilize free enzymes, which are not only easy to recycle and reuse, but also more facile to separate from the product [11–13]. Even so, there are still some potential limiting factors for the immobilized enzymes, including low loading efficiency, high leakage rate and mass transfer resistance [14]. Therefore, many efforts are needed to improve immobilization methods or synthesize new efficient carriers.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by the coordination between metal ions and organic ligands, are a series of porous crystals that have emerged as excellent carriers for immobilized enzymes because of tunable pore size, high surface area, and versatile framework composition [15,16]. MOFs with the larger surface area and tunable porosity properties enable the loading of more enzymes than conventional carrier materials. The shielding effect of the MOF framework allows the stabilization of the conformational structure of the enzyme, thus improving the stability of the enzyme [17]. Typically, immobilized enzymes on/in MOFs are prepared based on three types of strategies: in-situ encapsulation, surface immobilization, and pore entrapment with pre-synthetic MOFs. For in-situ encapsulation, enzymes can be encapsulated into the MOFs during the preparation process of MOFs [18–20]. The resultant enzyme@MOFs composites exhibit exceptional stability against some harsh conditions due to the shielding of MOFs. However, MOFs shell around the immobilized enzymes increases mass transfer limitation between the enzyme and substrates, and some organic precursors of MOFs also interact directly with enzymes, thus decreasing the catalytic activity [14,21,22]. For the pore entrapment with pre-synthetic MOFs, the diffusion of enzymes into MOFs pores is beneficial to improving enzyme loading and decreasing enzyme leaching. Nevertheless, the enzyme immobilization efficiency relies on the pore size of MOFs and the size of enzyme molecules. Therefore, this method is only suitable for mesoporous MOFs and enzymes with a small size. By contrast, surface immobilization is the most commonly used strategy for enzyme immobilization on MOFs. Enzymes can be adsorbed or bonded on the surface of MOFs [23,24]. However, enzymes are easy to detach from the surface of the MOFs, causing a significant loss of activity of the immobilized enzyme. Moreover, enzymes can hardly enter the pores of MOFs because most of the synthesized MOFs are micropores or small mesopores [25,26]. As a result, the surface immobilization on MOFs displays rather poor enzyme immobilization efficiency [27]. Especially, the immobilized enzymes on/in MOFs are difficult to recycle because of their small size and good dispersity in solvents. Many efforts have been performed to improve the catalytic performance of the immobilized enzyme on/in MOFs. As a type of MOFs, ZIF-8 synthesized by the coordination of 2-methylimidazole and zinc ions exhibits some interesting features, such as mild synthesis condition and high porosity, which makes ZIF-8 a common carrier for enzyme immobilization [20]. Sun and coworkers synthesized a hierarchically porous ZIF-8 by polyphenol treatment method to encapsulate enzymes. The resultant enzyme@ZIF-8 composites exhibited significantly increased stability [28]. Meso/microporous ZIFs were prepared to encapsulate lipase by a protein-induced softtemplating method. The activity recovery of the prepared lipase $@$ - ZIFs composites was increased six times [29]. A magnetic hierarchically porous $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ was synthesized by a modulatorinduced defect-formation strategy for amidase immobilization. The amidase@porous UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ composites displayed superior enzymatic activity and recyclability [30]. Despite performances of enzyme@MOFs composites were partially improved, the small pore size of MOFs was still a challenge for efficient enzyme immobilization.
Catalase (CAT) can catalyze the conversion of harmful hydrogen peroxide to water and molecular oxygen. Over the years, it has been studied and used in wound healing, antioxidant therapy, food industry, and biosensing. However, free CAT has a short shelf life, poor operational stability, and non-reusability, thus limiting its applications [31–33]. In this study, the novel magnetic and ordered 3D macroporous ZIF-8 was prepared to immobilize CAT. This process included the following steps: First, the assembled PS nanosphere monolith was used as a template to obtain the highly ordered macroporous ZIF-8 (MZIF-8). Subsequently, MZIF-8 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles were modified with 3- Aminopropyltrimethoxysilane (APTS), and the aminofunctionalized magnetic nanoparticles $\left(\mathsf{N F e}_{3}0_{4}\right)$ were connected with the amino-functionalized MZIF-8 (NMZIF-8) using glutaraldehyde (GA) as a bridge to achieve magnetic NMZIF-8 (mNMZIF-8). Finally, CAT molecules were precipitated and cross-linked on the surface of macroporous mNMZIF-8 and inside the macropores by adding ammonium sulfate (AS) and GA to obtain the immobilized CAT (CAT/mNMZIF-8). For comparison, we used the traditional ZIF8, MZIF-8, NMZIF-8, and magnetic amino-functionalized ZIF-8 (mNZIF-8) as the carriers to immobilize CAT by adsorption or covalent binding, respectively. We investigated the activity and stability of CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, CAT/mNZIF-8, and CAT/mNMZIF-8. As per our expectation, the CAT/mNMZIF-8 exhibited higher activity and immobilization efficiency than CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, and CAT/mNZIF-8.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Styrene $(99\%)$ , potassium persulfate $99\%$ , ${\ K}_{2}S_{2}{\ O}_{8}$ ), N, Ndimethylformamide (98%AR, DMF), $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(99\%)$ , $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(99\%)$ , and ammonium sulfate $98\%$ , AS) were purchased from Fu Chen Reagent Co., Ltd (Tianjin, China). Polyacrylic acid $(98\%)$ was obtained from Macklin Reagent Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Polyvinyl pyrrolidone (k-30, PVP), 2-methylimidazole $(98\%)$ , $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}\mathrm{-}$ $6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(98\%)$ , 3-Aminopropyltrimethoxysilane $97\%$ APTS), glutaraldehyde $50\%$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}0.$ , GA), catalase (EC 1.11.1.6 from bovine liver, $4\times10^{4}~\mathrm{U/mg}$ protein, CAT) and fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) and protein quantitative assay kit were purchased from Aladdin Reagent Inc. (Shanghai, China). All the other chemicals were of analytical grade.
## 2.2. Synthesis of 3D ordered PS (3DPS) template and MZIF-8
The 3DPS template was prepared according to Shen et al. [34]. Styrene was first washed thoroughly with $10\%$ NaOH solution and deionized water until the $\mathsf{p H}$ value is neutral. Then, $65~\mathrm{mL}$ styrene was added to $500~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water containing $2.5\ \mathrm{g}$ PVP, and stirred at $75\ ^{\circ}C$ for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ $(0.02~\mathrm{\g/mL)}$ was quickly added to initiate reaction at $75\ ^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the resultant mixture was PS spheres. Then the PS spheres were treated by ultrasonic, and centrifuged at
$2500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ . The white precipitates were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and used as a template to prepare MZIF-8.
For MZIF-8, $6.75\mathrm{~g~}$ of 2-methylimidazole and $\mathbf{8.15~g}$ of Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $45~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute methanol. A piece of 3DPS template was then soaked into the above mixture solution for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , and vacuum degassed for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The 3DPS were placed on a plate and dried at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The dried 3DPS were soaked in a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (volume ratio 1:1) mixture solution and treated with vacuum degassing for $3~\mathrm{min}$ , and dried at $50~^{\circ}C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and immersed again in DMF for $24\mathrm{h}$ to remove the PS template. Finally, the resultant MZIF-8 was washed with absolute methanol, and dried at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.3. Synthesis of $F e_{3}O_{4}$
$1.18~\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $0.695\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0.05\mathrm{~g~}$ of polyacrylic acid were simultaneously dissolved in $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. The pH value of the resultant mixture solution was adjusted to 9.0 by adding ${\sf N H}_{3}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ . After that, $5\mathrm{mL}$ polyacrylic acid solution $(0.14\mathrm{M})$ was added under ultrasonication at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The resultant black precipitates were recovered by a magnet, and washed with ethanol for three times, and dispersed in $35~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol at $4~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of $N F e_{3}O_{4},$ NZIF-8 and NMZIF-8
$\mathsf{N F e}_{3}0_{4}$ was prepared according to the Gao’s method [35]. Precisely, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ was added into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}}$ solution containing ${800}~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ APTS, and stirred at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was washed with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}}$ for several times and vacuum dried. For NZIF-8 and NMZIF-8, the amino-functionalized process is consistent with that of $\mathsf{N F e}_{3}0_{4}$ .
## 2.5. Preparation of immobilized enzymes
For CAT/ZIF-8 and CAT/MZIF-8, pure ZIF-8 was prepared first by our previous report [36]. Then, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of ZIF-8 or MZIF-8 was dispersed in $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ Tris-HCl buffer $^{'}100\ \mathrm{\mM},\ \mathrm{pH}\ 8.0)$ containing $0.56\mathrm{~mg~CAT}$ , and the mixture was placed in a shaker at $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 1 h. After centrifugation, the immobilized CAT was washed with deionized water for three times and suspended in $100\mathrm{mM}$ Tris-HCl buffer $\left(\mathrm{pH}8.0\right)$ .
For CAT/NMZIF-8, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of NMZIF-8 was added into $1~\mathrm{mL}$ TrisHCl containing $0.5\%$ (w/v) GA and incubated for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After centrifugation, the activated NMZIF-8 was washed with deionized water, and mixed with $1~\mathrm{mL}$ free CAT solution $(0.56~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to obtain CAT/NMZIF-8. After centrifugation, the CAT/NMZIF-8 was washed with deionized water, and suspended in $100\ \mathrm{mM}$ TrisHCl buffer $\left(\mathrm{pH}8.0\right)$ .
For $\mathrm{CAT/mNMZIF}–8$ and CAT/mNZIF-8, similar to previous reports with a few modifications [37,38], after the NMZIF-8 or NZIF-8 was activated by GA, a certain amount of the $\mathsf{N F e}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles were added into $100~\mathrm{{mM}}$ Tris-HCl buffer $\left(\mathsf{p H}8.0\right)$ 1 containing the activated NMZIF-8 or NZIF-8, respectively. After stirring for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ , the resultant mNMZIF-8 or mNZIF-8 was recovered by magnet, and washed with deionized water and freezing-dried. $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of mNMZIF-8 or mNZIF-8 was mixed with free CAT solution $(0.56~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ , and $50\%$ AS was added into the above mixture solution for $30\mathrm{min}$ . After that, $0.5\%$ GA was added for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . The resultant CAT/mNMZIF-8 or $\mathsf{C A T/m N Z I F{-}}8$ was recovered by a magnet, and washed with deionized water and suspended in $100~\mathrm{{mM}}$ Tris-HCl buffer $\left(\mathsf{p H8.0}\right)$ . The CAT protein amounts in the immobilized CAT were measured with a thermal gravimetric analyzer (TGA, NETZSCH). The protein loading capacity was defined as the ratio of CAT amount in the immobilized CAT to the weight of the immobilized CAT. The immobilization efficiency was defined as the ratio of CAT amount in immobilized CAT to the initial amount of CAT, using Eq. (1).
$$
\mathrm{Immobilizationefficiency(\%)}=\frac{(\mathrm{m}-\mathrm{C_{1}V_{1}})}{\mathrm{m}}\times100\%
$$
Here, m (mg) represents the mass of CAT initially added to the solution; $\hat{\mathbf{\alpha}}_{1}\:(\mathrm{mg/mL})$ and $\mathsf{V}_{1}$ (mL) are the CAT concentration and volume of the supernatant, respectively.
## 2.6. Characterization
## 2.7. Synthesis of labeled CAT with FITC
A certain amount of CAT was mixed with ${800\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ FITC solution $(6.25~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , dissolved in DMSO). After stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ without light, the unreacted FITC was removed by washing with ethanol. Then the labeled CAT with FITC was immobilized and observed by confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM, OLYMPUS FV1000).
## 2.8. Enzymatic activity assay
The activity of CAT was measured by the methods of the previous report [13]. Briefly, a certain amount of CAT samples and immobilized CAT were added into $2.5\mathrm{\mL}$ , 0.1 M Tris-HCl (pH 8.0) at $30~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ , $0.2\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added into the above mixture solution at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . After 7 min, $400~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $30\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ was added to terminate the reaction. The supernatant was collected, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations were determined by testing the decreasing absorbance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at $240\ \mathrm{nm}$ on an ultraviolet spectrophotometer. One unit of CAT activity was defined as the amount of enzyme that decomposes of $1\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ per min. The activity recovery of immobilized CAT was calculated with the Eq. (2).
$$
{\sf A c t i v i t y~r e c o v e r y(\%)}=\frac{\sf A1}{\sf A2}\times100\%
$$
A1 (U) and A2 (U) were the activity of immobilized CAT and the total free CAT activity used for immobilized CAT production, respectively.
## 2.9. Measurement of $K_{m}$ and $V_{m a x}$
The $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ values were obtained by determining the initial reaction rates with hydrogen peroxide [39]. The concentrations of hydrogen peroxide were ranged from $0.01\%$ , $0.03\%$ , $0.05\%$ , $0.1\%$ and $0.2\%$ with a reaction time of 7 min and the pretreatment conditions were the same as 2.8. The $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value and $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ values were calculated according to the Michaelis-Menten equation.
## 2.10. Reusability and storage stability
The reusability of immobilized CAT was measured by means of eight repetitive measurements using $0.2\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The immobilized
CAT was recovered and washed with $0.1~\mathrm{{M}}$ Tris-HCl $\mathtt{(p H8.0)}$ solution after each batch and then added to the next cycle. The storage stability of CAT and immobilized CAT were determined by incubating them at $25^{\circ}C$ and tested the activity every two days.
## 2.11. Statistical analysis
All the analytical experiments were performed in triplicates, and the results are the mean values of replicates along with standard deviation $(\pm\mathsf{S D})$ . The collected data were subjected to analysis of variance (ANOVA) using statistical analysis system software (IBM SPSS Statistics 21).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of CAT/mNMZIF-8
The synthesis procedure of CAT/mNMZIF-8 is presented in Fig. 1. The procedure included five steps: First, PS particles with a diameter of $270~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were prepared and used as templates. Subsequently, 3DPS were obtained by centrifuging the monodisperse PS solution. Second, the 3DPS were immersed in the mixture of imidazole and zinc ions, and induced by $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H/N H_{3}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ solution to accelerate the growth of ZIF-8 in 3DPS to obtain ZIF-8/PS composites. Third, the PS template was eliminated with DMF to form ordered macro-microporous MZIF-8. Fourth, MZIF-8 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ were modified with APTS, and used GA as a bridge to connect them to obtaining magnetic NMmZIF-8. Finally, CAT molecules were immobilized on the surface of macroporous NMmZIF-8 and inside the macropores by precipitation and cross-linking to form CAT/ NMmZIF-8. SEM images of PS microspheres, 3DPS, and MZIF-8 are presented in Fig. 2. The PS microspheres exhibited a uniform sphere shape with an averagediameter of about $270~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 2a). The 3DPS template showed a highly ordered structure (Fig. 2b). The uniform macropores with ordered arrangement were observed clearly in the MZIF-8 (Fig. 2c). The macroporous structure was further confirmed by TEM (Fig. 2d). Besides, TEM images of $\mathsf{N F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles and CAT/mNMZIF-8 are depicted in Fig. 2e and 2f, respectively. The $\mathsf{N F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles were discrete and spherical in shape (Fig. 2e), and successfully integrated into the ${\mathsf{C A T/m N M Z I F{-}}}8$ (Fig. 2f). Furthermore, TEM images of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ before and after APTES modification indicated that there were no obvious changes in morphology, and the diameter of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ was about $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figs. S1 and 2e).
The FITR spectra of ZIF-8, CAT/ZIF-8, MZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/ NMZIF-8, CAT/mNMZIF-8, and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were presented in Fig. 3a. The characteristic bands at $421~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1310\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1420~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ and imidazole ring stretching vibrations [40], which evidenced the presence of ZIF-8 in CAT/ZIF-8,
M ZIF 8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, and CAT/mNMZIF-8. The bands at $598\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the Fe-O group, only presented in CAT/mNMZIF-8 and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ , indicating the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ in CAT/mNMZIF-8 [41].
XRD was used to analyze the crystal structure of mNMZIF-8, NMZIF-8, MZIF-8, and ZIF-8. The mNMZIF-8, NMZIF-8, and MZIF
8 exhibited the same diffraction pattern with conventional ZIF-8 (Fig. 3b), indicating that the preparation process of mNMZIF-8, NMZIF-8, and MZIF-8 did not affect the crystal structure of ZIF-8.
$\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm of ZIF-8 and MZIF-8 showed the combination of type I and type IV isotherm characteristics, which indicate the presence of mesoporous and microporous structures of ZIF-8 and MZIF-8 (Fig. S2). The BET surface area and micropore volume of the MZIF-8 were $1805~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.58~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively. However, the BET surface area and micropore volume of ZIF-8 were only $739~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.27~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively (Table S1). Furthermore, the presence of macropores in MZIF-8 was confirmed by means of mercury intrusion porosimetry (Fig. 4a). The average pore diameter of the MZIF-8 was $69\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4b). However, the average pore diameter of the ZIF-8 was only $0.85~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Similar results were observed by Shen et al. [34].
To confirm the immobilization of CAT molecules on the supports, the labeled CAT with FITC was used to prepare immobilized CAT. CLSM images showed that green fluorescence was clearly observed in CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, and CAT/ mNMZIF-8 (Fig. 5), suggesting the successful CAT immobilization. However, CLSM images showed a clear difference in all the immobilized CAT. The labeled CAT molecules with FITC were not homogeneously distributed on the surface of CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8 and CAT/NMZIF-8 (Fig. 5a, 5b and 5c). By contrast, the labeled CAT molecules with FITC were distributed homogeneously on the surface and inside of the CAT/mNMZIF-8 (Fig. 5d). The results indicated that CAT molecules were immobilized on the surface and the internal macropores of CAT/mNMZIF-8 by precipitation and covalent cross-linking. Besides, the magnetization curves of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ , ${\sf N F e}_{3}0_{4}.$ , and CAT/mNMZIF-8 are displayed in Fig. 6. No obvious hysteresis was observed for $\mathsf{N F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and CAT/mNMZIF-8. The superparamagnetic behavior of CAT/mNMZIF-8 made the CAT/ mNMZIF-8 easy to recycle from the reaction mixture, indicating efficient recyclability.
## 3.2. Catalytic performance of CAT/mNMZIF-8
Activity recovery and immobilized efficiency of the CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, CAT/mNZIF-8 and CAT/mNMZIF-8 are listed in Table 1. Activity recovery of the CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, CAT/mNZIF-8 and CAT/mNMZIF-8 were $15\%$ , $16\%$ , $25\%$ , $35\%$ and $76\%$ , respectively. The activity recovery of the CAT/ mNMZIF-8 was 5.0 and 4.75 times higher than CAT/ZIF-8 and
CAT/MZIF-8, respectively. Highest immobilized efficiency was obtained in case of the CAT/mNMZIF-8. The enhanced activity recovery and immobilized efficiency of the CAT/mNMZIF-8 could be explained by the fact that mNMZIF-8 possesses highly order macropores. As a result, more CAT proteins can be precipitated and cross-linked on the surface of macroporous mNMZIF-8 and inside the macropores. Analysis results of TGA (Fig. S3) further demonstrated that the CAT/mNMZIF-8 $(29~\mathrm{wt\%})$ possessed a higher protein loading capacity than that of CAT/ZIF-8 $(1.2~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , CAT/MZIF8 (1 wt%), and CAT/mNZIF-8 $.11.8~\mathrm{wt\%})$ (Table S2). Besides, the water contact angle analysis showed that ZIF-8, MZIF-8, NMZIF8, and mNMZIF-8 were $64.6^{\circ}$ , $73.1^{\circ}$ , $38.3^{\circ}$ , and $40.1^{\circ}$ , respectively (Fig. S4). The results showed that ZIF-8 and MZIF-8 displayed higher hydrophobicity than NMZIF-8 and mNMZIF-8. The high hydrophobicity of ZIF-8 and MZIF-8 could cause the fact that the CAT molecules in aqueous solution were hard to diffuse into hydrophobic pores of ZIF-8 and MZIF-8, thus leading to low protein loading capacity and activity recovery. Previous reports demonstrated that the hydrophobic nature of ZIF-8 induces the aggregation of absorbed CAT molecules despite ZIF-8 had wettability [42]. Interactions between the hydrophobic ZIF-8 and hydrophobic groups on the enzyme surface could results in the loss of activity by changing the conformation of enzymes [43]. On the contrary, the low hydrophobicity of NMZIF-8 and mNMZIF-8 may be due to the presence of hydrophilic APTS on the surface of NMZIF-8 and mNMZIF-8 [44]. Furthermore, for CAT/mNMZIF-8, the conformation of the enzyme is more stable due to the formation of enzyme aggregates and multi-point chemical cross-linking among the enzyme molecules, thus retaining more activity.
$K_{\mathrm{m}}$ values play a key role in estimating the affinity of enzymes and substrates. Earlier reports showed that the mechanical agitation for free enzymes during the reaction process could cause partial unfolding and irreversible aggregation, resulting in an increase in $\mathtt{K}_{\mathtt{m}}$ values [45]. The $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value of the free CAT was lower than CAT/
ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, CAT/NMZIF-8, and CAT/mNMZIF-8 because of the enhanced mass transfer limitation of substrates toward the active enter of enzymes (Table 2). The CAT/mNMZIF-8 exhibited a higher $K_{m}$ value than CAT/NMZIF-8. This result could be explained due to the fact that high protein loading in the pore of mNMZIF-8 caused more mass transfer resistance of substrates. By contrast, the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value of CAT/mNMZIF-8 exhibited lower than CAT/ZIF-8 and CAT/MZIF-8. The improved mass transfer of CAT/ mNMZIF-8 came from the CAT on the surface of macroporous mNMZIF-8, which provided much easier accessibility of substrate to the enzymes. Importantly, CAT/mNMZIF-8 displayed a higher $V_{m a x}/K_{m}$ value than CAT/ZIF-8, CAT/MZIF-8, and CAT/NMZIF-8, indicating high catalytic efficiency. The reusability of the immobilized CAT was estimated and the results are displayed in Fig. 7. CAT/ZIF8, CAT/MZIF-8, and CAT/NMZIF-8 retained only $6\%$ , $27\%$ , and $21\%$ of their original activities after 6 cycles, respectively. However, the CAT/mNMZIF-8 was still maintained $90\%$ of its original activity even after eight consecutive operating cycles, indicating excellent reusability of the CAT/mNMZIF-8. The storage stability of CAT and immobilized CAT was investigated (Fig. 8). After 2 days of storage, CAT/ZIF-8 and CAT/MZIF-8 lost their activities. Nevertheless, the CAT/mNMZIF-8 preserved $60\%$ of its original activity after 9 days of storage. Obviously, the CAT/mNMZIF-8 had a better storage stability than the other immobilized CAT. The decreased activity of CAT/ZIF-8 and CAT/MZIF-8 could be the release of imidazole from the decomposition of ZIF-8 in the aqueous solutions. Previous reports proved that the protonation of imidazole from the decomposition of ZIF-8 in the aqueous solutions caused an increase in pH (alkaline), thus leading to the inactivation of enzymes [21,46]. On the contrary, the enhanced storage stability of the CAT/mNMZIF8 could be due to the formation of enzyme aggregates and multipoint cross-linking among enzyme molecules, thus increasing the rigidity of enzyme conformation and improving the resistance of enzymes to imidazole.
## 4. Conclusions
In a summary, a novel magnetic amino-functionalized ZIF-8 with 3D highly ordered macroporous structure was successfully synthesized using the assembled PS nanosphere monolith as a template. Enzyme molecules were immobilized on the surface of macroporous mNMZIF-8 and inside the macropores by precipitation, covalent binding and cross-linking. The resultant CAT/mNMZIF-8 exhibited superior immobilization efficiency and protein loading capacity, and displayed 5-fold higher activity than the traditional CAT/ZIF-8. Kinetic analysis showed that the CAT/ mNMZIF-8 possessed excellent catalytic efficiency. Furthermore, the CAT/mNMZIF-8 could be easily recycled by a magnet, and presented excellent reusability. To our knowledge, this is the first time to achieve the immobilized enzymes on/in ordered magnetic macroporous MOFs by precipitation, covalent binding and crosslinking. Moreover, the CAT/mNMZIF-8 displayed high protein loading capacity. The protein loading capacity of CAT/mNMZIF-8 was 2.9-fold and 5.8-fold higher than the conventional ZIF-8 and ZIF90 [22,47]. In conclusion, it is anticipated that this study could provide a facile and efficient approach to immobilize enzymes on/in MOFs with enhanced activity and excellent recyclability.
